The RSDP, the XSDT, the MADT and the IORT QEMU 11.1.1 published to its `virt`
machine under edk2-stable202408 (QEMU's own `edk2-aarch64-code.fd`), with no
operating system in the guest: the firmware was left to reach its shell, and
the guest's memory was then written out by the monitor's `pmemsave`. Every
address was walked host-side from the RSDP, found by its signature and both
checksums, so no byte here came through the decoder these fixtures judge. Each
table sums to zero.

  qemu-system-aarch64 -M <machine> -accel hvf -cpu host -smp 4 -m 256 \
      -nodefaults -display none \
      -drive if=pflash,format=raw,readonly=on,file=edk2-aarch64-code.fd

  hwgic/  -M virt,gic-version=3,iommu=smmuv3
          the hypervisor's own GICv3, which has no ITS: the MADT ends in a
          GIC MSI Frame structure, and the IORT is an SMMUv3 and a root complex
  its/    -M virt,gic-version=3,its=on,iommu=smmuv3,kernel-irqchip=off
          QEMU's emulated GICv3 with its ITS: the MADT ends in a GIC ITS
          structure, and the IORT is an ITS group, an SMMUv3 and a root complex

Both boots put every table at the same address, so the RSDP and the XSDT are
one file each:

  rsdp.bin  0x4cb43018      xsdt.bin  0x4cb43f18
  apic.bin  0x4cb43c98      iort.bin  0x4cb43198

Those are `tests/iort.rs`'s regions, so the XSDT's own entries point at them
and the walk under test is the real one. The XSDT's other six entries (FACP,
PPTT, GTDT, MCFG, SPCR, DBG2) name tables that are not here.
